Z||000000||Sootah||10-13-2001||08:20 PM||||As previously mentioned in my other rants I threw a rod on the way home after buying my 90,000 mile black 85 GT. Didn't see that one coming.<P>Where am I going to find a good running 2.8L Fiero engine? I was totally stoked about haveing this car only to get fùcked on the way home. I'm ever so depressed about it now. Where do all of you find yours at (those of you who have replaced them)? How much is this going to cost me?<P>Shìt...<P><B><I>-Disillusioned</B></I><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Nuclear_Happy_Face_Sig.jpg">     <BR><sup><I>Never pull your pants down in Cosco, the dang monkeys will getcha</I></sup> <BR><sup><B>AIM</B>: KSSouter</sup>||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||10-13-2001||08:34 PM||||I'll look around here for one, but plan on spending almost as much as you did for the car.  The last time I shopped around for Fiero V-6 engines the cheapest one was $500.  That was a complete engine though (alt., starter, exhaust manifolds, etc.), so you could recoup some of that $$ by selling the extra parts off of your engine.<BR>curious. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||dennis_6||10-13-2001||08:35 PM||||Sootah, I am in the same boat as you.  I think now would be a good time for a 3.4 conversion.  Since the block is about the same and the engines are much newer. (93-95).<BR>Other than that look for a wreck Fiero that still has engine that will fire up.  Otherwise you are problay gone have to rebuild one.||||reg||1
